complete_reproduction_plan:
  paper_info:
    title: "Functional Reward Encodings for Zero-Shot Offline Reinforcement Learning"
    core_contribution: "A method to pre-train a generalist agent from unlabeled offline trajectories using a transformer-based variational auto-encoder that encodes state-reward samples into a latent task representation, enabling zero-shot generalization to new downstream tasks via offline RL."

  file_structure: |
    project_root/
    ├── README.md                       # Documentation (implement LAST)
    ├── requirements.txt                # Dependencies (implement LAST)
    ├── main.py                         # Entry point: train and evaluate FRE
    ├── configs/
    │   ├── antmaze.yaml                # AntMaze environment config
    │   ├── exorl.yaml                  # ExORL environment config
    │   └── kitchen.yaml                # Kitchen environment config
    ├── fre/
    │   ├── encoder.py                  # Transformer VAE encoder (p_θ(z|·))
    │   ├── decoder.py                  # Reward decoder (q_θ(η(s)|s,z))
    │   ├── prior.py                    # Prior reward distribution sampling
    │   └── fre_trainer.py             # Phase 1: encoder-decoder training loop
    ├── rl/
    │   ├── iql.py                      # Implicit Q-Learning implementation
    │   ├── networks.py                 # Q, V, policy networks conditioned on z
    │   └── rl_trainer.py              # Phase 2: RL training with frozen encoder
    ├── data/
    │   ├── dataset.py                  # Offline dataset loading (D4RL, ExORL)
    │   └── replay_buffer.py           # Replay buffer for offline transitions
    ├── env/
    │   ├── antmaze.py                  # AntMaze environment & eval tasks
    │   ├── exorl.py                    # ExORL environment & eval tasks
    │   └── kitchen.py                  # Kitchen environment & eval tasks
    ├── eval/
    │   └── evaluate.py                 # Zero-shot evaluation on downstream tasks
    └── utils/
        ├── common.py                   # Helpers: logging, seeding, normalization
        └── discretization.py           # Reward discretization into embedding tokens

  implementation_components: |
    ============================================================
    COMPONENT 1: Prior Reward Distribution (fre/prior.py)
    ============================================================
    Purpose: Sample random unsupervised reward functions η ~ p(η) for training.
    Mixture of three types with equal probability (uniform mixture):

    a) Singleton Goal-Reaching Rewards:
       - Sample a goal state g uniformly from the offline dataset.
       - Reward: η(s) = 0 if ||s - g||_2 < ε (goal reached), else -1.
       - ε is a small threshold (e.g., 0.5 for AntMaze, tuned per domain).

    b) Random Linear Functions:
       - Sample a weight vector w ∈ R^d from Uniform(-1, 1) or N(0,1).
       - Apply sparse mask: randomly zero out a fraction (e.g., 50%) of entries.
       - Reward: η(s) = w^T s (dot product).

    c) Random MLPs:
       - Initialize a 2-layer MLP with random weights (Xavier uniform).
       - Architecture: input dim = state_dim, hidden dim = 256, output dim = 1.
       - Activation: ReLU. Weights frozen after initialization.
       - Reward: η(s) = MLP(s).

    Implementation: A class `PriorRewardDistribution` with method `sample()` that returns a reward function object (callable) and its type. The callable evaluates η(s) for a batch of states.

    ============================================================
    COMPONENT 2: Reward Discretization (utils/discretization.py)
    ============================================================
    Purpose: Map scalar rewards to learned embedding tokens for transformer input.
    - Define B bins (e.g., B=32) covering the expected reward range (e.g., [-1, 1]).
    - Learn an embedding matrix E ∈ R^{B × d_emb} (d_emb = transformer token dim).
    - For a scalar reward r, compute bin index: i = clamp(round((r - r_min) / (r_max - r_min) * (B-1)), 0, B-1).
    - Retrieve embedding e = E[i].
    - Alternatively, use a small MLP to predict embedding from scalar (if continuous embedding desired), but paper says "discretized according to magnitude into a learned embedding token space", so bin-based is correct.

    ============================================================
    COMPONENT 3: FRE Encoder (fre/encoder.py)
    ============================================================
    Purpose: Encode a set of K state-reward pairs into a latent vector z.
    Architecture: Permutation-invariant Transformer VAE.

    Input: Set of K tokens. Each token is concatenation of state s (dim d_s) and reward embedding e (dim d_emb). Total token dim = d_s + d_emb.
    - No positional encodings.
    - No causal masking (full self-attention).

    Transformer:
    - Number of layers L (e.g., 2-4), number of heads H (e.g., 4), feedforward dim FF (e.g., 512), dropout (e.g., 0.1).
    - Output: final layer representations of all K tokens.
    - Aggregate by averaging over tokens: h = (1/K) * Σ_i h_i.

    VAE heads:
    - Two linear projections from h to latent dim d_z:
        μ = Linear(h), log_σ = Linear(h)
    - Latent z sampled via reparameterization: z = μ + σ ⊙ ε, ε ~ N(0, I), σ = exp(log_σ).

    Output: z (dim d_z, e.g., 64 or 128), μ, log_σ.

    ============================================================
    COMPONENT 4: FRE Decoder (fre/decoder.py)
    ============================================================
    Purpose: Predict reward η(s) given state s and latent z.
    Architecture: Feedforward neural network (MLP).
    - Input: concatenation of state s and latent z.
    - Hidden layers: 2-3 layers, dim 256, ReLU activation.
    - Output: scalar predicted reward \hat{r}.
    - Trained jointly with encoder to minimize MSE: L_recon = (1/K') Σ (η(s_k^d) - \hat{r}_k)^2.

    ============================================================
    COMPONENT 5: FRE Training (fre/fre_trainer.py)
    ============================================================
    Purpose: Phase 1 - Train encoder and decoder jointly on random reward functions.
    Algorithm (from paper Algorithm 1, first while loop):
    ```
    while not converged:
        η ~ p(η)                                    # sample reward function
        {s_k^e}_{k=1..K} ~ D                        # K encoder states
        {s_k^d}_{k=1..K'} ~ D                       # K' decoder states (can use K'=K)
        r_k^e = η(s_k^e) for all k                  # compute rewards on encoder states
        tokens = [concat(s_k^e, embed(r_k^e))]      # build tokens
        μ, log_σ = encoder(tokens)                  # forward encoder
        z = reparameterize(μ, log_σ)                # sample latent
        \hat{r}_k^d = decoder(s_k^d, z) for all k   # predict rewards on decoder states
        L_recon = MSE(\hat{r}^d, η(s^d))            # reconstruction loss
        L_KL = KL( N(μ, σ) || N(0, I) )            # KL divergence to prior
        L_total = L_recon + β * L_KL                # β-VAE loss (β e.g., 0.1 or 1)
        update encoder and decoder parameters via Adam
    ```
    Hyperparameters: K=32 (as per paper), K'=32, β=0.1 (tuned), learning rate=1e-4, batch size of reward functions per step? Actually each step uses one reward function but multiple states. We can process multiple reward functions in a batch for efficiency: sample B_r reward functions, for each sample K states, compute loss averaged over B_r. Training steps: e.g., 100k-200k iterations.

    ============================================================
    COMPONENT 6: IQL Networks (rl/networks.py)
    ============================================================
    Purpose: Q-function, value function, and policy conditioned on latent z.
    All networks take state s and z as input (concatenated).
    - Q(s, a, z): MLP with input [s, a, z], output scalar Q-value.
    - V(s, z): MLP with input [s, z], output scalar value.
    - Policy π(a|s, z): Gaussian policy for continuous actions. MLP outputs mean and log_std. For discrete actions, softmax.
    Architecture: 2-3 hidden layers, dim 256, ReLU.

    ============================================================
    COMPONENT 7: IQL Algorithm (rl/iql.py)
    ============================================================
    Purpose: Offline RL algorithm used to train FRE-conditioned policy.
    Standard IQL (Kostrikov et al., 2021) adapted for z-conditioning:
    - Value function V(s, z) trained with expectile regression:
        L_V = E_{(s,a)~D} [ L2_τ ( Q_target(s,a,z) - V(s,z) ) ]
        where L2_τ(u) = |τ - 1(u<0)| * u^2, τ is expectile (e.g., 0.7-0.9).
    - Q-function Q(s,a,z) trained with standard TD:
        L_Q = E_{(s,a,r,s')~D} [ ( r + γ * V_target(s',z) - Q(s,a,z) )^2 ]
        Note: r = η(s) from sampled reward function, not from dataset.
    - Policy π(a|s,z) trained with advantage-weighted regression (AWR):
        L_π = E_{(s,a)~D} [ -exp( (Q(s,a,z) - V(s,z)) / τ_adv ) * log π(a|s,z) ]
        where τ_adv is temperature (e.g., 3.0).
    - Target networks for V and Q updated via Polyak averaging (τ_target=0.005).

    ============================================================
    COMPONENT 8: RL Training Loop (rl/rl_trainer.py)
    ============================================================
    Purpose: Phase 2 - Train IQL components with frozen FRE encoder.
    Algorithm (from paper Algorithm 1, second while loop):
    ```
    freeze encoder parameters
    while not converged:
        Sample batch of transitions (s, a, s') from offline dataset D (size B_rl)
        Sample reward function η ~ p(η)
        Compute rewards r = η(s) for the batch
        Sample K encoder states {s_k^e} ~ D
        Compute r_k^e = η(s_k^e)
        Encode z ~ encoder({ (s_k^e, r_k^e) })   # using frozen encoder
        # z is shared across the batch? Or per transition? Paper: "The latent representation z can then be used for RL training. The RL components ... are all conditioned on z." It seems one z per reward function is used for the whole batch. So we broadcast z to all transitions.
        Update Q(s,a,z), V(s,z), π(a|s,z) using IQL losses with r, s', z.
    ```
    Note: The paper uses a "strided training scheme": first train only encoder until convergence, then freeze and train RL. So we implement two separate loops.

    ============================================================
    COMPONENT 9: Evaluation (eval/evaluate.py)
    ============================================================
    Purpose: Zero-shot evaluation on downstream tasks.
    For each test task (defined in env/ files):
    - Sample K=32 states from offline dataset (or a fixed set of demonstration states).
    - Compute rewards using the test task's true reward function.
    - Encode to z using trained encoder.
    - Run policy π(a|s,z) in the environment for N_episodes (e.g., 20).
    - Compute average return (undiscounted sum of rewards).
    - Normalize returns to [0, 100] as per paper (details: min/max reference returns).
    - Report mean and std over 5 random seeds.

    Test tasks:
    - AntMaze: goal-reaching (multiple goals), directional (given (x,y) direction), random-simplex (procedural noise), path tasks (edges, loop, center).
    - ExORL: walker/cheetah goal-reaching (to random states), velocity (forward/backward).
    - Kitchen: 7 subtasks (microwave, light, etc.).

  validation_approach: |
    ============================================================
    Experiment 1: Zero-shot transfer on AntMaze
    ============================================================
    - Dataset: antmaze-large-diverse-v2 from D4RL.
    - Train FRE agent on prior reward distribution (mixture of three types).
    - Evaluate on: ant-goal-reaching, ant-directional, ant-random-simplex, ant-path-loop, ant-path-edges, ant-path-center.
    - Expected results (Table 1): FRE should achieve ~48.8 on goal-reaching, ~55.2 on directional, ~21.3 on random-simplex, ~67.2 on path-loop, ~60.0 on path-edges, ~64.4 on path-center. Overall antmaze-all mean ~52.8.
    - Compare with baselines if implemented (FB, SF, GC-IQL, GC-BC, OPAL) but primary goal is to reproduce FRE results.

    ============================================================
    Experiment 2: Zero-shot transfer on ExORL
    ============================================================
    - Dataset: ExORL walker and cheetah (exploratory data).
    - Train FRE agent.
    - Evaluate on: exorl-walker-goals, exorl-cheetah-goals, exorl-walker-velocity, exorl-cheetah-velocity.
    - Expected results: walker-goals ~94, cheetah-goals ~58, walker-velocity ~34, cheetah-velocity ~20. Overall exorl-all ~51.5.

    ============================================================
    Experiment 3: Zero-shot transfer on Kitchen
    ============================================================
    - Dataset: Kitchen environment from D4RL (kitchen-complete-v0 or similar).
    - Train FRE agent.
    - Evaluate on 7 subtasks.
    - Expected result: kitchen ~66.

    ============================================================
    Experiment 4: Ablation - Scaling with reward diversity (Section 5.3)
    ============================================================
    - Train agents on subsets of prior reward types (e.g., only goals, only linear, only MLPs, goals+linear, etc.).
    - Evaluate on all tasks; verify that FRE-all (full mixture) achieves highest total score.

    ============================================================
    Experiment 5: Domain knowledge augmentation (Section 5.4)
    ============================================================
    - Augment prior with domain-specific reward functions (e.g., XY-position only, velocity only).
    - Show improved performance on relevant tasks.

    ============================================================
    Success Criteria:
    - FRE agent achieves normalized returns within ±10% of reported values on major tasks.
    - Encoder training converges (MSE loss decreases, KL stabilizes).
    - RL training improves policy performance over time.
    - Zero-shot evaluation produces meaningful behaviors (visual inspection of trajectories).

